{
  "version": 1,
  "summary": "Adds verilex-agent, a launcher in its own agent/ module that runs the brain in a sandbox, lets it run only claim plans because verilex refuses its chains under --no-chain, and returns verilex's own verdict under one written verdict rule, with a CI check that keeps harness code out of the core.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "agent/internal/verdict/verdict.go",
      "summary": "Owns the verdict rule: README \"Verdict rule\" rows 9-33 turn the brain's runs, each planned by verilex, into one verdict.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            60,
            67
          ],
          "summary": "Request reads requested.chain, the field verilex fills with the caller's chain, or null when verilex planned the chain.",
          "rationale": "verilex is the one owner of what a chain argument is (F8). Under --no-chain no brain chain runs, so a set chain means that verilex ignored the flag. Verified by row 10 of TestVerdictRule and by TestRequestNamesTheCallersChain in the core.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            83,
            109
          ],
          "summary": "Parse refuses a claim run whose requested has no chain key, so an older verilex fails closed.",
          "rationale": "Without the key, the launcher cannot tell a run that verilex planned from a chain run. The check reads key presence, not value, because null is the planned value. Verified by TestClaimRunWithoutRequestedChainIsInconclusive and by the mutation that skips the check.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            121,
            186
          ],
          "summary": "Decide refuses a run that verilex did not plan, then returns the last red, then the budget, then no run, then the last run.",
          "rationale": "F11: a chain's run record set the word arguments of later claim plans in the home, so MAIN chose to let verilex refuse every brain chain under --no-chain. An unplanned run then means that verilex ignored the flag, so it is an environment failure, never a verdict. The stderr notes for brain chains are gone, because no chain runs. Verified by TestVerdictRule rows 9-33 and by mutations: an unplanned run judged (row 10), no final red (11, 14), no budget step (15, 16), no spec check (27, 28, 30) and no open claims (25) each fail the named rows.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            188,
            192
          ],
          "summary": "isPlanned is verilex's own reading of a run: the claim-run format, a requested object, and a null requested.chain.",
          "rationale": "A plain chain run has no format, and a chain given --changed has requested.chain set, so neither passes. Each row of TestVerdictRule also reads these three fields from every run record in the home (plannedOnly).",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            241,
            266
          ],
          "summary": "check refuses a green that did not prove a floor claim or was not asked about a changed path.",
          "rationale": "Every run here is a claim run, so check needs no other case. Rows 27, 28 and 30 cover each reason, and the mutation that skips check fails exactly those rows.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "agent/internal/run/run.go",
      "summary": "One launcher invocation: resolve the spec and its claim floor, snapshot the project, run the brain in the sandbox, check the project and home, return verilex's verdict.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            35,
            45
          ],
          "summary": "run.Timer starts a run's time budget, and WallClock, the timer the shipped launcher passes, is the wall-clock deadline Run used before.",
          "rationale": "F12: the budget also covers the intent lookup and the sandbox start, so a test with a fixed budget could not make the brain's run come before the end of the budget, and row 15 once tested the wrong case. Run now takes the timer from its caller, so a test can end the budget at a moment it picks, and the shipped behavior does not change. Verified by rows 14-16 of TestVerdictRule and by mutations: Run ignoring its timer, or starting it without the spec's budget, fails rows 14 and 15. WallClock never ending a budget fails rows 4 and 16.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            47,
            53
          ],
          "summary": "Result carries verilex's document, the exit code and the kept run directory, and no longer carries notes for brain chains.",
          "rationale": "No brain chain runs under --no-chain, so the launcher has nothing to report besides the verdict. AGENTS.md rule 5 (verdict first, failures only) holds.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            166,
            172
          ],
          "summary": "One deadline, started by the caller's timer, covers the intent lookup as well as both brain phases, and floor runs inside it.",
          "rationale": "N5: a hung verilex index used to hold the launcher with no limit, and the budget started only after it. Verified by row 4 of TestVerdictRule (a 1s budget ends a hung index in about one second) and the Lookup failures recipe.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            245,
            266
          ],
          "summary": "The launcher hands every run the proxy kept, and whether the budget ended, to verdict.Decide, and returns its verdict.",
          "rationale": "The rule has one owner. Rows 1-8 (spec, floor, sandbox, F1 and F5 checks) stay in Run, because they apply before any run is judged. With no verilex run and a failed brain, the reason names both.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            350,
            380
          ],
          "summary": "newLaunch builds the hide list: both homes, the ledger, the verilex binary, VERILEX_HOME, VERILEX_LEDGER, the run's logs and its verilex home.",
          "rationale": "These decide a verdict, so the brain must neither read nor write them. The ledger directory is created before the brain starts so the sandbox can hide it. Verified by the forgery test: every pass added to the ledger names a run the launcher made, and every earlier file is unchanged.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            395,
            445
          ],
          "summary": "brain passes the prompt as a file on stdin and writes the brain's output to the run's log files.",
          "rationale": "With no pipe, no copy goroutine can wait on a child the brain left running, which matters on macOS, where a detached process can outlive the run. Verified by TestBudgetBoundsTheRunWhateverTheBrainStarts (a setsid child) and the stdin check in TestSkillIntentAndSuggestionsReachTheBrain. A prompt larger than the pipe buffer is not tested.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            529,
            553
          ],
          "summary": "resolve gives verilex ticket at most 30 seconds, and a timeout is inconclusive.",
          "rationale": "N5 also named the ticket lookup. It cannot use the time budget, because the budget comes from the ticket. Verified on the real launcher by the Lookup failures recipe: a hung ticket ends after about 30 seconds with 'verilex ticket did not answer within 30s'. Not in the agent tests, because the test would wait 30 seconds.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            555,
            589
          ],
          "summary": "floor asks verilex index for the intent's claims within the budget, and a failure, a timeout, or no claim without --claim is inconclusive.",
          "rationale": "N6: an index failure is the environment's, so it says inconclusive, not refused. The floor must come from verilex, not from the brain. Verified by rows 1, 3, 4 and 28 of TestVerdictRule, and by the mutations that return the failure as a refusal (row 3 fails) or drop the budget (row 4 fails).",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            595,
            623
          ],
          "summary": "verilexJSON runs each lookup in its own process group with a 30-second deadline, and kills the group when the deadline or the budget ends.",
          "rationale": "If the deadline killed only the parent, a child that it started would keep the pipes open and hold the launcher. Killing the group ends both, and WaitDelay bounds the wait for the pipes. Verified by the Lookup failures recipe: after both hangs, pgrep finds no hung lookup left.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-agent-launcher"
        }
      ]
    },
